Highlighting the initial understanding of pointers!
Q1. What does int* p; actually create? What is in p right now?
Ans:- The "*" operator used during initialisation used along with data type is used to declare a pointer named p.
Right now since the p is only declared and not initialised the pointer has a grabage value. Uninitialise pointers are also called wild pointers.

Q2. What's the difference between p and *p?
Ans:- The difference between p and *p is that, p is a pointer the value it holds is a memory address of a given variable or some other object in the code.
 Whereas *p is used to get the object whose address is stored inside p. Here in this situation, in an expression the "*" operator works for dereferncing the pointer. *p behaves exactly as the object whose address it stores.

